WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.198
A FundsXML file has arrived.

2
00:00:02.248 --> 00:00:08.943
How does it get into the database, safely, even when it is large, and even when the same file arrives twice?

3
00:00:08.993 --> 00:00:15.562
In this film you see the steps of a robust loader, the traps on the way, and the reference loader at work.

4
00:00:16.812 --> 00:00:19.085
A robust loader works in six steps.

5
00:00:19.135 --> 00:00:23.002
Check the file: is it complete, is it the expected partner?

6
00:00:23.052 --> 00:00:25.060
Validate it against the schema.

7
00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.914
Register the delivery with its document ID.

8
00:00:27.964 --> 00:00:28.844
Read the data.

9
00:00:28.894 --> 00:00:31.698
Write it to the tables, in one transaction.

10
00:00:31.748 --> 00:00:35.814
And reconcile the result, before the data is released for use.

11
00:00:37.164 --> 00:00:39.051
It starts with a landing folder.

12
00:00:39.101 --> 00:00:45.529
New files arrive there, and the loader moves each file through clear states: in processing, done, or error.

13
00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:47.648
The original file is never changed.

14
00:00:47.698 --> 00:00:54.489
And every step writes a line to the log, so that later you can say exactly what happened to every file, and when.

15
00:00:55.839 --> 00:00:57.435
The first check is cheap.

16
00:00:57.485 --> 00:00:58.817
Is the file complete?

17
00:00:58.867 --> 00:01:02.702
A file cut off during transfer has no closing root element.

18
00:01:02.752 --> 00:01:07.178
Does it come from an expected sender, with an expected kind of data?

19
00:01:07.228 --> 00:01:09.614
Which schema version does it declare?

20
00:01:09.664 --> 00:01:11.721
Zipped files are unpacked first.

21
00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:14.354
Only then does the expensive work begin.

22
00:01:15.704 --> 00:01:17.273
When does the loader start?

23
00:01:17.323 --> 00:01:22.909
Either a watcher notices every new file and starts at once, or the loader runs at fixed times.

24
00:01:22.959 --> 00:01:29.684
The best of both: load as soon as a file arrives, and raise an alarm when a file has not arrived by its deadline.

25
00:01:29.734 --> 00:01:35.200
Then a late delivery is noticed in the morning, not at noon, when someone asks for the data.

26
00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:38.261
Validate first, load later.

27
00:01:38.311 --> 00:01:45.498
A file that fails the schema is not loaded at all, not even partly, because half a delivery is worse than none.

28
00:01:45.548 --> 00:01:49.606
The error report is kept with the file, and the sender is told.

29
00:01:49.656 --> 00:01:55.279
Remember that FundsXML needs an X S D one point one validator, such as Saxon or Xerces.

30
00:01:56.629 --> 00:01:58.609
Which schema to validate against?

31
00:01:58.659 --> 00:02:02.423
Against the version the file declares, as long as you know it.

32
00:02:02.473 --> 00:02:09.681
When a partner already sends a newer version, validate against that one, load the elements you know, and log the rest.

33
00:02:09.731 --> 00:02:15.463
The original file is in the archive, so nothing is lost; the new elements can be loaded later.

34
00:02:16.813 --> 00:02:22.231
Files arrive twice more often than you think: a repeated transfer, a manual upload.

35
00:02:22.281 --> 00:02:24.998
So the loader registers every document ID.

36
00:02:25.048 --> 00:02:28.028
If the same ID comes again, it skips the file.

37
00:02:28.078 --> 00:02:33.101
If a delivery is a correction, it replaces the data of the delivery it names.

38
00:02:33.151 --> 00:02:37.119
That way, loading the same file twice never doubles the data.

39
00:02:38.469 --> 00:02:44.679
Another question to settle with each partner: does a delivery contain the full state, or only the changes?

40
00:02:44.729 --> 00:02:49.286
A full delivery replaces everything the receiver knows for that fund and date.

41
00:02:49.336 --> 00:02:53.243
A delta delivery only adds and changes, and needs a complete start.

42
00:02:53.293 --> 00:02:57.022
Full deliveries are simpler and more robust; deltas save volume.

43
00:02:57.072 --> 00:02:59.739
Your loader must know which one it is reading.

44
00:03:01.089 --> 00:03:02.382
How to read the file?

45
00:03:02.432 --> 00:03:05.834
The simplest way loads the whole document into memory.

46
00:03:05.884 --> 00:03:08.712
That is fine for prices and small portfolios.

47
00:03:08.762 --> 00:03:16.384
But a delivery with thousands of positions, regulatory templates and embedded documents can reach hundreds of megabytes.

48
00:03:16.434 --> 00:03:22.011
Then read it as a stream, element by element: with StAX in Java, or iterparse in Python.

49
00:03:23.361 --> 00:03:24.860
Streaming has one catch.

50
00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:29.830
In FundsXML, the asset master data comes after the funds and their positions.

51
00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:33.444
So while reading a position, its asset is not known yet.

52
00:03:33.494 --> 00:03:36.607
Two solutions: read the file twice, assets first.

53
00:03:36.657 --> 00:03:41.835
Or write positions into a staging table and link them to their assets at the end.

54
00:03:41.885 --> 00:03:44.223
The reference loader does the second.

55
00:03:45.573 --> 00:03:47.853
This is the core of the reference loader.

56
00:03:47.903 --> 00:03:55.750
It walks through the file and handles one position at a time: it reads the identifiers and values, and writes the row to the staging table.

57
00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:00.125
After each position the memory is released, so the file size does not matter.

58
00:04:01.475 --> 00:04:02.304
Then the keys.

59
00:04:02.354 --> 00:04:08.835
For each asset, the loader looks for it by its ISIN, or by the key built from sender and own identifier.

60
00:04:08.885 --> 00:04:10.845
If it is unknown, it creates it.

61
00:04:10.895 --> 00:04:14.927
If it is known, it updates its master data with the newer values.

62
00:04:14.977 --> 00:04:21.584
Every new asset is logged, because a sudden flood of new assets often points to a problem in the delivery.

63
00:04:22.934 --> 00:04:25.646
Writing happens in one transaction per file.

64
00:04:25.696 --> 00:04:30.730
If anything goes wrong, everything is rolled back, and the database is as before.

65
00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:33.994
For speed, rows are written in bulk, not one by one.

66
00:04:34.044 --> 00:04:41.965
And after the cause of an error has been fixed, the file is simply loaded again; thanks to the document ID, nothing is doubled.

67
00:04:43.315 --> 00:04:46.173
Not every problem is a reason to reject a file.

68
00:04:46.223 --> 00:04:47.720
A schema error always is.

69
00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:54.713
But a business warning, such as an unusually large price change, may be correct; load the data, flag it, and ask.

70
00:04:54.763 --> 00:05:03.190
A real business error, such as weights that add up to a hundred and twenty percent, holds the delivery back until the sender corrects it.

71
00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:05.665
Agree on these rules with your partners.

72
00:05:07.015 --> 00:05:08.176
How fast can it be?

73
00:05:08.226 --> 00:05:14.803
With bulk inserts, or the copy command of PostgreSQL, a loader writes thousands of positions per second.

74
00:05:14.853 --> 00:05:19.137
Large tables load faster when some indexes are built after the load.

75
00:05:19.187 --> 00:05:24.744
And processing one fund at a time keeps memory small and lets you restart in the middle.

76
00:05:24.794 --> 00:05:27.867
Speed is rarely the real problem; correctness is.

77
00:05:29.217 --> 00:05:40.089
Here the loader runs over the four realistic example files: one delivery each, registered with its document ID, with funds, share classes, prices, positions and assets.

78
00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:41.909
A second run skips all four.

79
00:05:41.959 --> 00:05:47.565
The positions table now holds the portfolio of the multi-asset fund, ready for queries.

80
00:05:48.915 --> 00:05:51.388
Before the data is released, reconcile it.

81
00:05:51.438 --> 00:05:54.632
Do the position values add up to the total net assets?

82
00:05:54.682 --> 00:05:57.335
Do the weights add up to one hundred percent?

83
00:05:57.385 --> 00:06:00.759
Are the counts plausible compared with the last delivery?

84
00:06:00.809 --> 00:06:06.946
The data-quality checks of FreeXmlToolkit do the same on the file; the loader repeats them on the data.

85
00:06:08.296 --> 00:06:09.910
The loader also checks codes.

86
00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:16.625
Every listed code, such as a price type or a document type, is compared with the lookup tables of the schema version.

87
00:06:16.675 --> 00:06:20.183
A code from a newer version than yours is kept with a warning.

88
00:06:20.233 --> 00:06:25.922
Any other unknown code is an error, because it usually means a mapping problem on the sender's side.

89
00:06:27.272 --> 00:06:31.022
When a delivery fails, the sender needs a report it can act on.

90
00:06:31.072 --> 00:06:38.743
It names the file and its document ID, the rule that failed, the path of the element and the value found, and what was expected.

91
00:06:38.793 --> 00:06:43.388
And it says what happens now: the file was rejected, or loaded with warnings.

92
00:06:43.438 --> 00:06:47.731
A precise report gets a correction back in minutes, a vague one in days.

93
00:06:49.081 --> 00:06:52.011
Test the loader as carefully as the generator.

94
00:06:52.061 --> 00:06:54.020
Use the official example files.

95
00:06:54.070 --> 00:07:00.110
Add broken ones: a file cut off in the middle, an invalid file, a file from an unknown sender.

96
00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:04.063
Load the same file twice, then a correction, then a deletion.

97
00:07:04.113 --> 00:07:05.747
And try a very large file.

98
00:07:05.797 --> 00:07:10.477
The reference implementation does several of these tests on every change.

99
00:07:11.827 --> 00:07:13.685
Only then is the data released.

100
00:07:13.735 --> 00:07:18.177
A status column on the delivery shows the way: loaded, checked, released.

101
00:07:18.227 --> 00:07:21.562
Reports and downstream systems read only released data.

102
00:07:21.612 --> 00:07:29.500
Warnings from the reconciliation go to the data team, who decide whether to release anyway or to ask the sender for a correction.

103
00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:38.315
That was loading FundsXML: validate first, never twice, streaming for large files, and reconcile before release.

104
00:07:38.365 --> 00:07:43.885
In the next film, you see the other direction: generating FundsXML from a database.

105
00:07:43.935 --> 00:07:45.227
Thanks for watching.
